WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:10.000
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: May we then move on straight away Simon to you for the chief executives report

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:12.180
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Thank you Malcolm

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:18.200
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: I just would start by underlining
something that we said last week, which was

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:24.750
to place on record our thanks to the staff
of the NHS in London, who responded to the

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:30.710
Westminster attack so effectively and, in
the case of the staff from St Thomas', running

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:38.620
across into the line of danger bravely. I
think the NHS response across London has been,

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:45.160
as you would expect, magnificent, and I think
we owe a debt of gratitude not just to the

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:50.600
paramedics, but to the doctors, the nurses
and others who have been involved in looking

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:55.980
after the people arising from that attack.
We have said that before, but I think it is

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:58.320
right to record that here in this public meeting.

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:08.610
More prosaically, the Executive Group has
been out and about around the country, and

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:13.970
so we have our recent Executive Group meetings
since we last met in various places, including

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:22.320
Hull and Whitstable. I have had the pleasure
of attending three Public Accounts Committees

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:29.030
since we were last together, discussing health
and social care integration; GP access, which

00:01:29.030 --> 00:01:38.780
we will talk about with Ros this morning;
and yesterday, Jane and I did a session on

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:48.360
progress on learning disability services.
I think that was a good discussion that reported

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:52.790
the views of the NAO that we agreed with;
that frankly, there had not been enough progress

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:58.700
between 2012 and 2015, but since 2015, we
have really begun to get some momentum in

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:04.120
that programme. I think we agreed with their
assessment, and had a good conversation with

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:06.000
the PAC on that yesterday.

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:13.000
In terms of other things that I have been
up to, obviously out and about talking to

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:20.260
the BMA General Practitioners Committee; talking
to some of the life sciences companies, particularly

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:26.530
around new medicines; on health and work in
the West Midlands with Sue at the Chief Scientific

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:30.579
Officers Conference; and so forth. I think
the substance of what we have been doing,

00:02:30.579 --> 00:02:36.230
frankly, as an organisation has been trying
to get the 2017/2018 operating plans to the

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:40.959
best place we can, and I think we are in a
much better position than we were last year,

00:02:40.959 --> 00:02:44.840
although there are still significant gaps
in some parts of the country that need to

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:51.120
be worked through; and framing our overall
work plan for the next two years in the context

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:57.430
of the next steps on the NHS Five Year Forward
View document, which is being published tomorrow,

00:02:57.430 --> 00:03:17.579
and we will have a chance to talk about that
later on so I will not go through that now.

00:03:17.579 --> 00:03:28.650
The framing of this is that, as the paper
says, we are required to publish our annual

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:34.629
Business Plan by the end of March each year.
Tomorrow is indeed the end of March, and therefore,

00:03:34.629 --> 00:03:41.310
tomorrow, we will be publishing this document,
but it goes wider than simply describing the

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:45.879
actions that NHS England will be taking to
fulfil the requirements that the government

00:03:45.879 --> 00:03:51.999
set in the mandate; it is a broader system
response to the challenges facing the NHS

00:03:51.999 --> 00:03:58.180
right now, and it sets out not only the practical
steps that we aim to take over the next two

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:04.180
years to improve services in key areas of
greatest concern to the public, but focuses

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:08.790
much more on the what and the how rather than
the why of how we are going to bring about

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:17.079
that change. Given that it is published tomorrow,
obviously I am not going to break that by

00:04:17.079 --> 00:04:22.160
going into the detail here today. The Board
have had a chance to discuss the content on

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:29.470
several occasions now, so this obviously is
a collective endeavour. I do not know what

00:04:29.470 --> 00:04:32.560
else, Malcolm, you want to cover at this point.

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:36.470
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: The only thing I would emphasise
is the governance bit, because it is really

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:41.930
quite hard to get formal governance around
a document which is chameleon-like, changing

00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:47.310
some of its detail - not its key messages,
but much of its detail - over time. Just to

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:54.450
reassure more generally that first, we took
at our last meeting a decision that sign-off

00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:58.900
would be by the Chief Executive and the Chair
and Deputy Chair, and that is exactly what

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:04.940
will occur. Second, we put on that some conditions
about engagement, and we have certainly observed

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:09.130
that. I am grateful to all the non-executive
and executive directors of the Board for their

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:13.500
input, and for their assurance that we are
now in the position where we have a document

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:19.660
that we feel is fit for publication; and more
than that, is a positive contribution to future

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:22.230
thinking about the NHS.

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:27.240
Throughout this, there has been extensive
engagement and discussion with our various

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:31.310
stakeholders, as I was saying before, so I
think in governance terms, I would just like

00:05:31.310 --> 00:05:37.350
the Board to take some assurance that we are
at a position of formal compliance with where

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:39.890
we should be.

00:05:39.890 --> 00:05:50.000
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Simon, clearly the delivery
plan is being launched tomorrow. I think one

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:58.310
of its purposes is to mobilise the NHS in
aggregate behind the critical priorities that

00:05:58.310 --> 00:06:03.790
have been set, both in the mandate and from
the various other elements. That involves,

00:06:03.790 --> 00:06:10.150
I think, engagement with our people as well
as with citizens, and I am intrigued as to

00:06:10.150 --> 00:06:17.680
what the process is by which we communicate
with and engage the people of the NHS so that

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:23.100
everyone is clear about what we are trying
to do, where we are marching and, importantly,

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:29.460
the contribution at all levels that everyone
can make to help take those priorities forward.

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:36.370
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Yes, so there has obviously
been a process hitherto in that there are

00:06:36.370 --> 00:06:44.480
various inputs to the framing of these priorities;
as we know, the mandate that Malcolm has described,

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:53.500
and in it this year, realism about the likely
improvement trajectories around A&amp;E, four-hour

00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:59.500
waits, RTT and so on, so we are reflecting
those judgements that are made in the mandate.

00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:05.250
Over and above that, we obviously do direct
work with the public, with patient groups,

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:10.420
and a number of them have been involved in
formulating this, to the extent we are giving

00:07:10.420 --> 00:07:15.280
practical effect over the next two years to
the mental health task force, the cancer task

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:20.970
force, the urgent and emergency care redesign:
they have already been the subject of big

00:07:20.970 --> 00:07:26.480
engagement, so we are putting our best foot
forward on turning those plans into reality.

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:34.830
Then Healthwatch undertakes an annual prioritisation
exercise, drawing on the Healthwatch branches

00:07:34.830 --> 00:07:40.080
around the country, that identifies the top
things they think that the public want the

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:45.350
NHS to focus on, so we have factored those
in as well. It will not be a surprise to know

00:07:45.350 --> 00:07:51.340
that improvements in mental health services
and access to GPs, and better working between

00:07:51.340 --> 00:07:56.650
hospitals and the local community health services
and social care, were their top priorities

00:07:56.650 --> 00:08:00.250
the second year in a row, so that, again,
is part of the reason why we are focusing

00:08:00.250 --> 00:08:05.850
so clearly on those. Obviously, this is all
in the framing in the run-up to tomorrow.

00:08:05.850 --> 00:08:09.040
I think your question was as much about what
happens after tomorrow as it was about where

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:10.040
we get to.

00:08:10.040 --> 00:08:11.330
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: My question was what happens
afterwards, exactly.

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:18.010
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Obviously, there is a large
communications exercise to do with CCGs and

00:08:18.010 --> 00:08:21.840
trusts, but also, we have been working with
a number of voluntary and community-sector

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:27.224
partners on this, and are looking to re-energise
some of the commitments that were set out

00:08:27.224 --> 00:08:32.310
in the original Five Year Forward View around
how the change process has to be more than

00:08:32.310 --> 00:08:36.700
just the statutory organisations and the NHS,
and as part of what we are going to be saying

00:08:36.700 --> 00:08:42.479
in terms of the detail on how STPs are going
to develop, being clear about the broader

00:08:42.479 --> 00:08:47.649
local government role there, not just the
social care aspects of that. So, there is

00:08:47.649 --> 00:08:54.439
a whole multitude of effort which is required,
and there are actions that some of the individual

00:08:54.439 --> 00:08:59.310
ALBs, national leadership bodies themselves
will be taking: HEE publishing a workforce

00:08:59.310 --> 00:09:01.480
plan for England, for example, next month.

00:09:01.480 --> 00:09:06.660
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Presumably there will be
a cascade within NHS England for its own people

00:09:06.660 --> 00:09:13.060
to engage them in the thinking, and therefore,
again, how each and every person within the

00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:17.399
organisation can help make, in their role,
a contribution to delivery?

00:09:17.399 --> 00:09:24.420
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Yes, that is right. Both
Jane and Bruce will be involved with their

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:31.490
respective professional groups and stakeholders.
There is an aspect of this which is reminding

00:09:31.490 --> 00:09:35.550
people in laying out the big reform agenda
that there is actually a nursing workforce

00:09:35.550 --> 00:09:42.350
that has emerged over the last six to 12 months.
In the case of clinical leadership and front-line

00:09:42.350 --> 00:09:48.220
medicine, there are some practical things
that we want to do to try and make junior

00:09:48.220 --> 00:09:53.889
doctors' lives easier, and so Bruce and colleagues
have been involved in developing those with

00:09:53.889 --> 00:10:01.300
our partners, so that is in here, as well.

